    "domain-expansion": {
      verified: true,
      first: "The system is formally explained during Gojo's battle with Jogo.",
      source: "Domain_Expansion",
      sections: [
        {
          title: "Core structure",
          paragraphs: [
            "Domain Expansion materializes a user's innate domain and imbues it with a cursed technique. A conventional completed domain encloses the target inside a barrier, creates an environment ideal for the user, and grants the imbued technique a guaranteed-hit effect.",
            "The visual environment is not merely decoration. It expresses the user's inner world and the logic of the technique while giving that technique its most advantageous possible conditions."
          ]
        },
        {
          title: "What 'sure-hit' means",
          paragraphs: [
            "A sure-hit does not necessarily mean instant death. It means the domain's programmed effect reaches the target according to the domain's rules without needing an ordinary projectile or attack trajectory.",
            "Some domains are lethal and directly attack the target. Older-style or rule-based domains can instead force participation in a trial, game, or other system. The guaranteed effect depends on the user's technique and barrier design."
          ]
        },
        {
          title: "Domain clashes",
          paragraphs: [
            "When domains overlap, their barriers and sure-hit effects compete. Refinement is the most famous factor, but cursed-energy output, barrier skill, compatibility, and the exact construction of each domain can all matter.",
            "If one domain is overwhelmingly superior, it can suppress the other. If they are close enough, the sure-hits may cancel while the barriers continue to struggle, turning the clash into a fight over who can maintain their domain and damage the other user first."
          ]
        },
        {
          title: "Costs and weaknesses",
          bullets: [
            "Domain Expansion consumes a huge amount of cursed energy.",
            "After the barrier ends, the user's cursed technique is normally unstable or temporarily unusable because of technique burnout.",
            "A barrier can be attacked from outside, where conventional domains are often less resistant because they are designed to trap targets inside.",
            "Simple Domain, Hollow Wicker Basket, and Falling Blossom Emotion can counter or reduce sure-hit effects in different ways, but none is a universal victory button."
          ]
        },
        {
          title: "Open-barrier domains",
          paragraphs: [
            "Sukuna and Kenjaku demonstrate domains that manifest their technique directly in real space without closing a conventional outer shell. This is treated as an extraordinary barrier feat.",
            "Leaving an escape route can function as a binding vow that greatly expands effective range. An open domain also interacts differently with closed barriers because it can attack the outside of the opponent's shell."
          ]
        },
        {
          title: "Common misconceptions",
          bullets: [
            "A Domain Expansion is not automatically stronger just because its user has more cursed energy.",
            "Not every domain has an instant-kill sure-hit.",
            "An incomplete domain can still provide major environmental advantages even without a completed barrier or sure-hit.",
            "Using a domain does not permanently remove a cursed technique; the usual consequence is temporary burnout."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },

